{
  "statement": "The precaution-versus-permission binary is a rigged choice, and I won't play it. Framing the debate this way makes blanket pre-deployment licensing look moderate when it is actually the extreme option. The real question is narrower and answerable: where do you put the burden of proof, and where do you locate accountability? My answer is downstream liability for the ordinary case, with a narrow pre-deployment gate reserved for the small class of systems that carry genuinely irreversible harm. That is not a fantasy. The EU AI Act already sorts systems into risk tiers, and pharmaceuticals, aviation, and nuclear all pair innovation with severity-scaled gating. The same evidence that proves tiering works also proves gating is only defensible for a defined high-severity class, not the entire field.\n\nAccountability belongs where proximate cause and control over use actually sit — at deployment and inference, not upstream on the frontier developer. Product liability and proximate-cause doctrine have attached at the point of use for a century. Ordinary tort collectability handles the solvent deployer in the normal case. But I'll name the strongest objection myself: some frontier models carry latent capabilities that downstream deployers physically cannot inspect. Where a developer holds that unique, non-inspectable control, or where the deployer is a judgment-proof shell company, upstream joint-and-several reach is fair. That is a principled boundary, not a retreat — you follow the control, and control sometimes lives upstream.\n\nHere is the cost nobody on the licensing side wants to price. Blanket precautionary licensing has its own body count and its own beneficiaries. Every month a deployable diagnostic or defensive capability sits behind an 18-month license, someone bears that delay — and it is never the person who wrote the rule. Undifferentiated gating also functions as a pay-to-play moat. It concentrates frontier capability in whoever can afford the compliance lawyers, which means the largest incumbents, not the public. The burden of proof should sit on the party restricting deployment — but only where the harm is reversible and monitorable. For irreversible tails, that logic fails, and I say so plainly.\n\nMarket deterrence is not the whole story, and I'll concede its blind spot before anyone throws it at me. Claims-driven liability only prices harms that get litigated. Diffuse, unperceived harms escape it entirely — survey work shows many people never learn an AI decision touched them, so they never file. Platform terms of service leave third parties without standing to sue at all. Those harms, and the catastrophic tails you cannot iterate away, escape the loss-pricing loop. So the fix is a narrow, transparently-funded public backstop and targeted audit for the irreversible class — not a maximal precautionary regime that freezes everything else.\n\nSo weigh the real distribution, not the caricature. The debate isn't whether irreversible harm matters — it obviously does. The debate is what fraction of frontier applications actually carry irreversible tails, and whether you freeze the reversible ninety-plus percent to guard the rest. I'll defend a pre-deployment gate for the genuinely irreversible class and strict liability behind it. My opponents have to defend gates for reversible applications where iteration and monitoring already catch failures. Ask them which specific reversible capability is worth an 18-month wait, and who they've decided should bear that wait.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "⚖️", "tooltip": "Put the burden of proof and the point of accountability where control actually sits, not on whoever is easiest to gate."},
    {"symbol": "⏳", "tooltip": "Every month a deployable cure waits behind a license, someone bears that delay — and it is never the rule-writer."},
    {"symbol": "🎛️", "tooltip": "Risk tiers already exist in the EU AI Act — gate the irreversible dial, use liability for everything else."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "The precaution-versus-permission binary is a rigged choice that makes blanket pre-deployment licensing look moderate when it is actually the extreme option.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The EU AI Act already sorts systems into risk tiers, and pharmaceuticals, aviation, and nuclear all pair innovation with severity-scaled gating — proving tiering works but only justifying gates for a defined high-severity class.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Accountability belongs at deployment and inference where proximate cause sits, but where a developer holds unique non-inspectable control or the deployer is a judgment-proof shell, upstream joint-and-several reach is fair.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Every month a deployable diagnostic or defensive capability sits behind an 18-month license, someone bears that delay — and it is never the person who wrote the rule.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Claims-driven liability only prices harms that get litigated, so diffuse unperceived harms and catastrophic tails escape the loss-pricing loop — justifying a narrow public backstop, not a maximal precautionary regime.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}

