ng to do with safety. They scale favorably for whoever can staff an audit team and absorb the paperwork — the incumbents already holding the compute. 'Foreclosing unproven benefits' is an abstraction that names no one. So let me name it: the mechanism doesn't stop innovation in the ether, it stops the firm that can't afford a compliance department. The people funding a risk-mitigation regime are systematically not the people it protects. And Anthropic's ASL tiers are a self-imposed structure with no external enforcement — safety as a press release, not a constraint. I'll flag my own exposure here: incumbent entrenchment is a structural prediction to test, not proven capture. But the burden sits with anyone proposing a license to show it won't become a drawbridge.\n\nThere's a third path, and it already exists — the barrier is political will, not feasibility. The NIST AI Risk Management Framework, released in January 2023, and ISO 42001 have real adoption right now. Gate auditing to demonstrated capability instead of self-attestation. Then deputize private litigants to enforce below federal jurisdiction, so the enforcement channel reaches the unperceiving victim that a federal license — written by the firms lobbying it — will never touch. This distributes enforcement outward rather than concentrating it in one gate the incumbents write. So here's my question for both of you: your preferred mechanism, whichever it is — does it put a dollar in the hand of the renter who never learned an algorithm rejected them, or does it just make you feel safer while she stays in the dark?",
