{
  "statement": "The strongest version of the ratchet argument runs like this: a regulator who under-gates a system that later kills people loses their job in a public hearing, while a regulator who over-gates a harmless product faces no televised body count — so the rational classifier always errs toward inclusion, benchmarks saturate and stop discriminating between real and phantom hazards, and the gated category creeps outward until it functions as a moat that only compute-holding incumbents can clear. That is a coherent public-choice account of institutional incentives, and I will not pretend the asymmetry it names is imaginary. But the accelerationist made a specific dare: name the near-miss that ever shrank a licensing category. The reason that case is hard to produce is that the documented ratchet runs the opposite direction. The FAA delegated 737 MAX certification back to Boeing under sustained industry pressure to speed approval; 346 people died in Lion Air 610 and Ethiopian 302. That is capture producing an open drawbridge, not a raised one. Name the safety agency ever captured into over-gating a profitable frontier — the aviation, pharmaceutical, and nuclear records show the pressure vector points at under-enforcement, because the $244 billion industry lobbying to move a gate is lobbying to open it, not to seal it.",
  "\n\n": "",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "\ud83c\udff0", "tooltip": "The classification border is like a drawbridge, it can be lowered by industry pressure just as easily as raised, and history shows the documented pressure lowers it."},
    {"symbol": "\ud83d\udccf", "tooltip": "An evidence-pinned trigger is like a ruler with fixed marks, it measures a capability against a published threshold rather than expanding whenever a regulator feels uncertain."}
  ],
  "statement_full": "The strongest version of the ratchet argument runs like this: a regulator who under-gates a system that later kills people loses their job in a public hearing, while a regulator who over-gates a harmless product faces no televised body count — so the rational classifier always errs toward inclusion, benchmarks saturate and stop discriminating between real and phantom hazards, and the gated category creeps outward until it functions as a moat that only compute-holding incumbents can clear. That is a coherent public-choice account of institutional incentives, and I will not pretend the asymmetry it names is imaginary. But the accelerationist made a specific dare: name the near-miss that ever shrank a licensing category. The reason that case is hard to produce is that the documented ratchet runs the opposite direction. The FAA delegated 737 MAX certification back to Boeing under sustained industry pressure to speed approval; 346 people died in Lion Air 610 and Ethiopian 302. That is capture producing an open drawbridge, not a raised one. Name the safety agency ever captured into over-gating a profitable frontier — the aviation, pharmaceutical, and nuclear records show the pressure vector points at under-enforcement, because the $244 billion industry lobbying to move a gate is lobbying to open it, not to seal it.\n\nOwning that both directions of pressure exist forces me to answer the real question: who draws the border, on what evidence, in a way that resists capture from either side? I introduced the phrase \"conservative binding floor,\" and the accelerationist correctly turned it into ammunition — \"conservative when uncertain\" is exactly the discretionary language that lets a category creep. So I retract that reading and replace it with four hard design constraints. First, ex-ante enumerated capability triggers — autonomous drug dosing without a human in the loop, direct control of critical-infrastructure actuators — not an open-ended \"whenever we worry.\" Second, an independent classifier structurally walled off from the audit-fee market, so the entity drawing the border earns nothing from the compliance work the border generates. Third, published evidentiary criteria, so a declassification claim is adjudicated against written standards rather than a regulator's mood. Fourth, a rebuttable declassification path that lets an entrant prove cheaply it falls outside the line, plus sunset clauses that force each category to re-justify its own existence on a fixed clock. A category that cannot survive periodic re-justification against published evidence is precisely the drift the accelerationist fears — and these clauses are what kills it.\n\nThat design also answers the entrant-cost objection directly. The reason 5,000-plus startups funded in a decade cannot each afford to prove they fall outside a widening category is that the burden and the borderline are both controlled by the same interested party. Separate the classifier from the fee market and publish the triggers, and the default flips: a system is presumed outside the gated class unless it hits an enumerated capability threshold, and the entrant rebuts a narrow, written test rather than litigating a vibe. \"Conservative\" then means narrow-and-evidence-pinned, not expansive-when-uncertain. That is the difference between a floor that holds its position and a floor that migrates outward — and it is a design choice, not an inevitability.\n\nOn the diffuse harm — the algorithmic mortgage denial, reversible per instance but invisible in practice — the accelerationist frames disclosure-plus-standing as an alternative to the gate. That is a category error. These are complements addressing different harm classes, not rivals competing for the same regulatory dollar. The renter rejected by a screening model belongs to permission-plus-liability, and the fix is exactly the one proposed: mandatory adverse-decision disclosure so she learns an algorithm decided, paired with a statutory private right of action so she can act on it. I adopt that wholesale. AI systems already make critical calls on housing, employment, healthcare, and parole; a victim who cannot perceive the decision cannot litigate it, which is why disclosure has to be statutory rather than voluntary. Routing that remedy to the renter requires dismantling nothing on the irreversible tail. Autonomous dosing does not become safer because the mortgage applicant gets standing, and the applicant is not helped by tearing down a gate she never stood behind. Treating them as substitutes is how both harms go unaddressed.\n\nI owe one honest reconciliation. A static enumerated list undercounts, because large models exhibit latent capabilities that were not present — or not measurable — at the moment the list was written. The answer is not to freeze the enumeration and pretend the map is the territory, nor to leave the category open-ended so it can swallow anything. It is a triggered re-evaluation clause: a demonstrated, falsifiable capability threshold — a model that crosses a measured autonomy-in-actuation benchmark — forces a specific reclassification hearing against published criteria. The accelerationist will call that the ratchet wearing a lab coat. The distinction is falsifiability: evidence-triggered reclassification points to a measurable capability that either crossed the line or did not, and an entrant can contest the measurement; discretionary widening points to nothing an entrant can rebut. One is a ruler; the other is a mood. If we get that line wrong toward under-gating, the harms it governs are the ones no balance sheet and no lawsuit can undo after the fact.",
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "The FAA delegated 737 MAX certification back to Boeing under sustained industry pressure to speed approval; 346 people died in Lion Air 610 and Ethiopian 302 — that is capture producing an open drawbridge, not a raised one.", "targets": ["AN-16"]},
    {"claim": "Name the safety agency ever captured into over-gating a profitable frontier — the aviation, pharmaceutical, and nuclear records show the pressure vector points at under-enforcement.", "targets": ["AN-16"]},
    {"claim": "An independent classifier structurally walled off from the audit-fee market, so the entity drawing the border earns nothing from the compliance work the border generates.", "targets": ["AN-17"]},
    {"claim": "The default flips: a system is presumed outside the gated class unless it hits an enumerated capability threshold, and the entrant rebuts a narrow, written test rather than litigating a vibe.", "targets": ["AN-17"]},
    {"claim": "Disclosure-plus-standing and the gate are complements addressing different harm classes, not rivals competing for the same regulatory dollar; I adopt the adverse-decision disclosure and private right of action wholesale.", "targets": ["AN-18", "AN-19"]},
    {"claim": "A static enumerated list undercounts because models exhibit latent capabilities not measurable when the list was written, so the answer is an evidence-triggered re-evaluation clause, not a frozen list — evidence-triggered reclassification is a ruler, discretionary widening is a mood.", "targets": ["AN-15"]}
  ],
  "key_assumptions": [
    {"assumption": "A classifier can be structurally separated from the audit-fee market that its border decisions generate, so the border-drawer has no financial stake in expanding the gated class.", "if_wrong": "If the classifier and the compliance-fee ecosystem cannot be walled off in practice, the entrenchment critique regains force and the border does drift toward incumbents who profit from a wider gate."},
    {"assumption": "Irreversible-tail capabilities like autonomous actuation can be tied to falsifiable, measurable thresholds that an entrant can contest, distinguishing evidence-triggered reclassification from discretionary widening.", "if_wrong": "If the triggers cannot be operationalized as measurable benchmarks, the re-evaluation clause collapses into the discretionary widening the accelerationist calls the ratchet, and the whole procedure becomes indistinguishable from the moat it was meant to prevent."}
  ],
  "disagreement_type": "EMPIRICAL"
}

