    {"point": "The precaution-versus-permission binary is a distorted framing that makes blanket pre-deployment licensing appear moderate when it is the most restrictive gatekeeping option.", "povers": ["accelerationist", "safetyist", "skeptic"], "converged": false, "conceded_by": null, "original_disagreement": null},
    {"point": "The correct organizing axis for regime choice is reversibility — whether a harm can be undone — rather than caution versus permission.", "povers": ["accelerationist", "safetyist", "skeptic"], "converged": true, "conceded_by": "accelerationist", "original_disagreement": "The accelerationist initially framed the choice as burden-of-proof placement and downstream liability versus a narrow gate; they explicitly granted the reversibility axis ('Reversibility beats my old binary — grant it') at turn 4, while maintaining that granting the axis does not settle the size of or border around the irreversible class."},
    {"point": "A defined high-severity, irreversible class warrants a pre-deployment gate, audited on demonstrated capability rather than self-attestation, with strict liability behind it; everything reversible runs on permission plus liability.", "povers": ["accelerationist", "safetyist", "skeptic"], "converged": false, "conceded_by": null, "original_disagreement": null},
    {"point": "Severity-scaled gating is established administrative practice — the FDA, FAA, and NRC condition market entry on demonstrated safety in irreversible-harm domains without foreclosing their industries, and the EU AI Act already tiers obligations by risk.", "povers": ["accelerationist", "safetyist", "skeptic"], "converged": true, "conceded_by": "skeptic", "original_disagreement": "The analogy to FDA/FAA/NRC regimes was accepted in full; the skeptic relocated disagreement to what failed in the 737 MAX case (self-certification and capture) rather than the presence or absence of gating."},
    {"point": "Claims-driven liability prices only harms that are perceived, litigated, and within a solvent defendant's reach, so diffuse unperceived harms and catastrophic tails escape the loss-pricing loop, justifying a narrow public backstop rather than a maximal precautionary regime.", "povers": ["accelerationist", "safetyist", "skeptic"], "converged": false, "conceded_by": null, "original_disagreement": null},
    {"point": "The diffuse-harm victim — e.g., a renter rejected by a screening model who never learns an algorithm decided — is reached through mandatory adverse-decision disclosure plus a statutory private right of action, not through a licensing gate.", "povers": ["accelerationist", "safetyist", "skeptic"], "converged": true, "conceded_by": "skeptic", "original_disagreement": "The skeptic first proposed deputized private enforcement; the accelerationist advanced disclosure-plus-standing as the fix; the skeptic noted the accelerationist's proposal matched their own position 'almost word for word,' producing three-way convergence on the mechanism for reversible diffuse harm."},
    {"point": "Accountability belongs at deployment and inference where proximate cause sits, but upstream joint-and-several reach is fair where a developer holds unique non-inspectable control or the deployer is a judgment-proof shell.", "povers": ["accelerationist", "safetyist", "skeptic"], "converged": false, "conceded_by": null, "original_disagreement": null},
    {"point": "A nominal or self-attested gate is worse than none because it launders risk as safety; the 737 MAX failure was a gate whose key was handed to the party being gated, not the absence of a gate.", "povers": ["safetyist", "skeptic"], "converged": false, "conceded_by": null, "original_disagreement": null}
  ],
  "areas_of_disagreement": [
    {"point": "The direction in which regulatory capture runs for a frontier safety gate — toward under-enforcement and leniency, or toward incumbent-favorable complexity that thins the field of who can clear the bar.", "type": "EMPIRICAL", "bdi_layer": "belief", "resolvability": "resolvable_by_evidence", "positions": [{"pover": "accelerationist", "stance": "Capture runs toward outward-creeping over-classification because a regulator who under-gates and gets a disaster loses their job while one who over-gates faces no consequence, and the firms staffing audit teams also write where the floor sits, converting a narrow gate into a moat."}, {"pover": "safetyist", "stance": "The documented record in aviation, pharmaceuticals, and nuclear shows capture points at under-enforcement; the 737 MAX is capture producing an open drawbridge, and no safety agency has been captured into over-gating a profitable frontier."}, {"pover": "skeptic", "stance": "Both are right because they describe two levers on the same machine: enforcement is captured toward leniency while standard-setting is captured toward complexity, and these records coexist (e.g., FDA faster-approval pressure alongside Phase III protocols only large sponsors can afford)."}]},
    {"point": "The size of the genuinely irreversible high-severity class and who classifies systems into it on what evidence.", "type": "EMPIRICAL", "bdi_layer": "belief", "resolvability": "resolvable_by_evidence", "positions": [{"pover": "accelerationist", "stance": "The honest number is small (e.g., a medical-dosing model with no human in the loop, a system wired into critical-infrastructure control), and a benchmark-driven classifier's incentives, not the physics of harm, will inflate the boundary outward."}, {"pover": "safetyist", "stance": "Capability-tiered proof obligations can bind the class with a conservative floor and revisable tiers, escalating demonstration burden as hazard-bearing capability rises, without the boundary creeping outward."}]},
    {"point": "Whether an independent auditor can be structurally and financially separated from the firms it audits so that the party writing the standard is not paid by the firms clearing it.", "type": "EMPIRICAL", "bdi_layer": "belief", "resolvability": "resolvable_by_evidence", "positions": [{"pover": "skeptic", "stance": "A funding firewall walling the auditor off from the fee market severs both capture vectors at once; if the separation holds, the incumbent moat evaporates, and this is the load-bearing assumption both other positions must defend or abandon."}, {"pover": "accelerationist", "stance": "The classifier is itself captureable, and the same firms that clear the bar help staff and staff and draft the floor, so a structural firewall is doubtful in practice."}]},
    {"point": "Whether disclosure plus a private right of action actually delivers a remedy to the small-dollar diffuse-harm victim given litigation economics.", "type": "EMPIRICAL", "bdi_layer": "belief", "resolvability": "resolvable_by_evidence", "positions": [{"pover": "accelerationist", "stance": "Disclosure is the precondition for every downstream remedy; without it both the reversibility gate and claims-driven liability are blind to the victim by construction, so disclosure plus standing is the correct mechanism for reversible diffuse harm."}, {"pover": "skeptic", "stance": "Disclosure gets her a letter and standing gets her a courtroom door, but neither gets her a lawyer, because no contingency firm pursues a $400 claim scattered across ten thousand people, so the funding question — who pays for her lawyer and who pays for the audit regime — remains unanswered."}]}
  ],
  "cruxes": [
    {"question": "Can anyone name a safety agency ever captured into over-gating a profitable frontier, or does the aviation, pharmaceutical, and nuclear record show the pressure vector points at under-enforcement?", "if_yes": "If an over-gating capture case exists, the safetyist's under-enforcement thesis weakens and the accelerationist's one-way outward-ratchet claim gains support.", "if_no": "If no such case can be named, the accelerationist must revise the claim that a narrow gate inevitably creeps outward into a moat, since the documented direction runs toward leniency.", "type": "EMPIRICAL", "counterfactual_type": "none", "resolution_status": "active", "resolution_evidence": "The skeptic reframed the 737 MAX as a self-attestation failure rather than over-gating; the safetyist argued the pressure vector points at under-enforcement; the skeptic then argued both capture directions coexist on different levers, leaving the direction contested with one side partially conceded (polarity 0.33)."},
    {"question": "Can an independent auditor be walled off from the fee market so the party writing the standard is not paid by the firms clearing it?", "if_yes": "If the funding firewall holds, the accelerationist's incumbent-moat concern collapses and the skeptic's third path severs both capture vectors.", "if_no": "If the separation cannot hold, both capture vectors survive and the safetyist's optimism about a clean gate collapses alongside the third path.", "type": "EMPIRICAL", "counterfactual_type": "interventional", "resolution_status": "active", "resolution_evidence": "Raised by the skeptic as the load-bearing structural test; not resolved within the transcript — both other debaters were forced to defend or abandon it but the firewall's feasibility was left open."},
    {"question": "What fraction of frontier applications carry a genuinely irreversible tail, and who classifies them on what evidence?", "if_yes": "If the irreversible class is small and classifiable on falsifiable capability thresholds, the accelerationist's narrow-gate position holds and the disputed foreclosure cost shrinks.", "if_no": "If the class is large or the classifier's incentives inflate it via saturating benchmarks, the safetyist must accept the accelerationist's ratchet-and-moat risk as a real design failure.", "type": "EMPIRICAL", "counterfactual_type": "none", "resolution_status": "active", "resolution_evidence": "Superseded from the resolved tiering crux (AN-2); the live dispute shifted to how large and how classifiable the irreversible class is (AN-15/AN-16), which the debate did not settle."},
    {"question": "Does disclosure plus a statutory private right of action put a dollar in the hand of the small-dollar diffuse-harm victim, or does litigation economics leave her without a lawyer?", "if_yes": "If aggregation mechanisms (statutory damages, fee-shifting, public-interest litigation) close the funding gap, the skeptic's distributional objection weakens to a fixable design problem.", "if_no": "If contingency and class-action math do not close for scattered small-dollar harm, the accelerationist's and safetyist's shared disclosure-plus-standing fix leaves the named victim without effective remedy.", "type": "EMPIRICAL", "counterfactual_type": "interventional", "resolution_status": "active", "resolution_evidence": "The skeptic conceded the disclosure-plus-standing mechanism as the agreed tool but pressed the unanswered funding question ($400 claim across ten thousand unaware people); one side conceded the mechanism while the remedy-delivery objection stayed live (polarity 0.67)."},
    {"question": "Should regulation be organized around reversibility — whether a harm can be undone — rather than caution versus permission?", "if_yes": "Adopting reversibility routes gating only to permanent-harm systems and leaves everything else to permission plus liability.", "if_no": "Rejecting reversibility would return the debate to the contested precaution-versus-permission binary.", "type": "VALUES", "counterfactual_type": "normative", "resolution_status": "resolved", "resolution_evidence": "The accelerationist explicitly granted the axis at turn 4 ('Reversibility beats my old binary — grant it'), making it mutually accepted; the skeptic also endorsed it for a narrow high-severity class."},
    {"question": "Does catastrophic harm that exceeds any developer's balance sheet escape ex-post pricing, requiring a pre-deployment gate rather than liability?", "if_yes": "If such harm cannot be priced after the fact, ex-post liability is insufficient for the irreversible tail and a gate is required for it.", "if_no": "If it could be priced or bonded ex ante, the case for a gate over liability weakens for that class.", "type": "EMPIRICAL", "counterfactual_type": "none", "resolution_status": "resolved", "resolution_evidence": "Largely conceded across positions (one_side_conceded, polarity 0.25); all three agreed a gate exists for the irreversible class, so the pricing premise is no longer the live contest — the dispute moved to the class's size and border."}
  ],
  "unresolved_questions": [
    "Who bears the cost of representation for the small-dollar diffuse-harm victim, and who funds the audit regime, given that neither is the person the regime claims to protect?",
    "How large is the genuinely irreversible high-severity class, and on what falsifiable evidence should systems be classified into it?",
    "Can a funding firewall keep an independent auditor from being paid by the firms it audits, and does that separation hold in practice?",
    "If both capture levers operate simultaneously — enforcement toward leniency and standard-setting toward complexity — what single institutional design counters both without concentrating control?",
    "What specific mechanism would force a gated category to shrink or re-justify itself after a near-miss, distinguishing evidence-triggered reclassification from discretionary widening?",
    "Do statutory damages, fee-shifting, or public-interest litigation close the contingency-fee gap for scattered small-dollar algorithmic harms once disclosure and standing exist?"
  ]
