    {
      "label": "Scoping Irreversible-Harm Classification Boundaries",
      "description": "A Belief within accelerationist discourse concerning the size of the genuinely irreversible high-severity AI class and the incentives governing who classifies systems into it. Encompasses: the empirical smallness of applications carrying irreversible tails (e.g., autonomous medical-dosing, critical-infrastructure control), the role of classifier incentives versus harm physics in setting the boundary, and benchmark saturation as a driver of category creep. Excludes: the reversibility axis itself as an organizing principle, and downstream liability allocation mechanisms.",
      "pov": "accelerationist",
      "category": "Beliefs",
      "rationale": "The debate repeatedly turned on the size of the irreversible class and who draws its border as the decisive unresolved crux, distinct from whether reversibility is the correct axis; no existing node captures the classification-boundary contest.",
      "source_claims": ["C8", "C9", "C21"]
    },
    {
      "label": "Bidirectional Regulatory Capture Dynamics",
      "description": "A Belief within skeptic discourse holding that enforcement capture and standard-setting capture operate as separate levers, the former pulling toward leniency and the latter toward incumbent-favorable complexity. Encompasses: coexistence of under-enforcement and complexity-driven field-thinning in comparable regimes such as pharmaceutical faster-approval pressure alongside costly Phase III protocols, and the resulting synthesis of opposing capture-direction claims. Excludes: single-direction capture accounts, and self-attestation failure treated as an isolated design flaw.",
      "pov": "skeptic",
      "category": "Beliefs",
      "rationale": "The skeptic dissolved the accelerationist/safetyist dispute over capture direction by positing two simultaneous capture vectors, a framing not represented by any single-direction capture node.",
      "source_claims": ["C15", "C9", "C12"]
    },
    {
      "label": "Closing Diffuse Small-Dollar Remedy Gaps",
      "description": "A Desire within skeptic discourse targeting delivery of an actual remedy to victims of dispersed low-value algorithmic harm. Encompasses: the litigation-economics shortfall where disclosure and standing fail to secure counsel for a scattered small-dollar claim, and the funding question of who pays for both the victim's lawyer and the audit regime. Excludes: catastrophic-tail pricing, and mechanisms addressed only to perceived high-value harms.",
      "pov": "skeptic",
      "category": "Desires",
      "rationale": "The debate surfaced an unresolved gap where disclosure-plus-standing convergence still leaves the diffuse small-dollar victim without a viable remedy due to contingency and class-action economics; no node captures this remedy-delivery gap.",
      "source_claims": ["C16", "C18", "C20"]
    },
    {
      "label": "Firewalling Auditors from Audited Firms",
      "description": "An Intention within skeptic discourse to structurally and financially separate the party writing safety standards from the firms paying to clear them. Encompasses: severing the leniency lever by separating grader from graded and the complexity lever by preventing incumbents from drafting the floor they profit from raising, and the feasibility test of naming a funding firewall. Excludes: self-attestation regimes, and licensing gates concentrated in a single body funded by applicants.",
      "pov": "skeptic",
      "category": "Intentions",
      "rationale": "The funding-firewall proposal was presented as the load-bearing structural test both other positions must defend or abandon, refining the existing performance-gated auditing node with a specific financial-separation mechanism.",
